<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- MAS flavor: helper sidecar entitlements (clauge-server inside
         Contents/Helpers/Clauge Helper.app/). Applied via post-build
         codesign in scripts/build-mas-clean.sh.

         Split-entitlements pattern: helpers/sidecars omit
         application-identifier + team-identifier so they don't trip
         Transporter 90885 (nested executable profile mismatch). The main
         bundle keeps the full entitlements + embedded provisioning profile.

         v0.9.10 timeline of three landings on the right configuration:

         (1) v0.9.0 build 3: sidecar at Contents/MacOS/clauge-server with
             full main entitlements (including app-sandbox). Standalone
             Mach-O has no embedded Info.plist → libsystem_secinit's
             _libsecinit_appsandbox.cold.6 SIGTRAPs (no
             kCFBundleIdentifierKey). Apple rejected under Guideline
             2.1(a) ("app does not load its content after launch").

         (2) Commit cd83087 (v0.9.10 mid-cycle): removed app-sandbox from
             sidecar entitlements. Runtime worked (sidecar inherited
             parent's sandbox via process tree because no own container).
             Transporter rejected the .pkg with HTTP 409 — its static
             validator hard-requires app-sandbox=true on every Mach-O in
             a MAS bundle.

         (3) Helper.app wrap + app-sandbox=true (re-added) + own
             Info.plist with CFBundleIdentifier=com.clauding.clauge.helper.
             FIRST attempt: tried to give the helper its own per-binary
             container. _libsecinit_appsandbox.cold.9 SIGTRAPped with
             asiSignatures SYSCALL_SET_USERLAND_PROFILE — secinitd's
             kernel syscall to apply the helper's sandbox profile failed
             because a sandboxed parent can't spawn a sandboxed child
             with a NEW container under the parent's local-test
             entitlement set. Crash report
             clauge-server-2026-05-28-230755.ips.

         (4) Helper.app wrap + com.apple.security.inherit=true (this
             config). The inherit entitlement tells the kernel "attach me
             to the parent's existing sandbox rather than creating a new
             one." Transporter still sees app-sandbox=true on the Mach-O
             (passes static validation). libsystem_secinit skips per-
             binary container setup (no SIGTRAP). The helper inherits the
             parent's startAccessingSecurityScopedResource grant on
             ~/.claude/, so the SEA Node server can read Claude Code
             logs without an app-groups migration. This is the
             Apple-documented pattern for sandboxed bundled helpers;
             Chrome's `Google Chrome Helper.app` and every Electron
             renderer use it. Reference: Apple Technical Note TN2206. -->
    <!-- app-sandbox: required for MAS submission; satisfied at runtime
         because the helper.app's Info.plist provides CFBundleIdentifier. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- inherit: THIS is the load-bearing entitlement for the helper.app
         pattern. Tells the kernel: "don't set up a fresh container for me;
         inherit the parent's sandbox state instead." With `inherit=true`:
           - Transporter still sees app-sandbox=true on the Mach-O (passes
             static validation).
           - libsystem_secinit skips per-binary container setup at runtime
             (no _libsecinit_appsandbox.cold.9 SIGTRAP — secinitd just
             attaches the helper to the parent's existing sandbox).
           - The helper inherits the parent's
             startAccessingSecurityScopedResource grant on ~/.claude/, so
             the SEA Node server can read the user's real Claude Code
             logs without a separate app-groups container migration.
         This is the Apple-documented pattern for sandboxed bundled
         helpers; Chrome's `Google Chrome Helper.app` and every Electron
         renderer use it. Without it, a helper.app inside a sandboxed
         parent fails secinitd's profile-set syscall (asiSignatures
         SYSCALL_SET_USERLAND_PROFILE — observed locally 2026-05-28
         23:07 BDT, crash report clauge-server-2026-05-28-230755.ips). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.inherit</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- cs.allow-jit: required for the SEA Node binary's V8 JIT. NOT a
         sandbox entitlement — it's a code-signing hardening flag, not
         affected by inherit. Has to be on the helper's own signature
         because the dyld JIT permission check runs per-binary. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Network + files entitlements that used to live here have been
         REMOVED. With `inherit=true`, the helper picks them up from the
         parent (`entitlements.mas.plist` declares network.client +
         network.server + files.user-selected.read-only +
         files.bookmarks.app-scope). Listing them on the helper too would
         be redundant at best and a potential conflict signal to App
         Review at worst. Reference: Apple Technical Note TN2206, Chrome
         /Electron helper entitlements convention. -->

</dict>
</plist>
